    "collected_at": "2026-11-13T17:28:46.365371+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/1968-shelby-mustang-gt350-flaunts-special-order-paint-true-one-of-one-pony-278386.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/1968-shelby-mustang-gt350-flaunts-special-order-paint-true-one-of-one-pony-278386.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-11-13T14:21:31+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-13T14:21:31+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-13T17:30:19.490207+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-13T17:30:19.490207+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\n1968 Shelby Mustang GT350 Flaunts Special-Order Paint, True One-of-One Pony\n24 photos\nPhoto: American Mustangs/YouTube\nIntroduced in 1965, the GT350 was the very first Mustang to wear a Shelby badge. Originally a track-prepped vehicle , the GT350 eventually morphed into a more conventional pony car. Joined by the GT500 in 1967, the GT350 remained in production through 1969, when Ford and Shelby parted ways.\nWhile the regular Mustang was very popular at the time, the Shelby GT350 was produced in limited numbers. Shelby put together a little over 7,000 units over five model years, with a few hundred leftover cars sold in 1970. The figure includes over 1,000 GT350H Hertz rent-a-racers.\nWith the exception of the first model year, when only 562 units were built, the Sheby GT350 moved over 1,000 units a year. The 1968 fastback you see here is one of 1,457 examples made, which is a lot of cars for a golden-era muscle car. It's also one of 1,053 fastbacks, as well as one of only 528 vehicles fitted with the four-speed manual transmission.\nIt becomes rarer when we start discussing options, but there's one feature that makes this Shelby truly special. If you're familiar with Shelby Mustang, you probably already spotted it because it's in plain sight. Yes, I'm talking about the color. This bright orange hue makes this Shelby GT350 unique.\nIt's not because it was an unpopular color; this pony car was commissioned with a special-order paint. It shows up on the invoice, which also lists the color code as WT5014. The color is called Mustang on the said invoice, but it's actually one of the Ford Mustang's more iconic hues. It just didn't become an option until later.\nThe color was added to the regular palette for the 1970 model year. It was called Grabber Orange, joining a high-impact trio that also included Grabber Blue and Grabber Green.\nSo what's the deal with this orange color initially called Mustard? Where did it come from? Well, its origins are unclear, but it was most likely taken from Ford's fleet car color palette, as was the case with many special-order cars at the time.\nLike most high-profile Mustangs, this Shelby GT350 has a detailed Marti Report. It states that three cars were finished in this special-order hue. The other two are GT500KR models, so we're indeed looking at the only GT350 out there. While a lot of cars are one-of-one classics thanks to a combination of options, mainly decided by items like a radio or other insignificant thing, this Shelby is as genuine as they get.\nIn addition to being unique, this Shelby is also in stunning condition. The fastback looks showroom-new, meaning it probably received an extensive restoration a while back. Both the paint and the Deluxe interior look flawless, while the engine bay is detailed to Concours level.\nSpeaking of the engine bay, it houses a 302-cubic-inch (4.9-liter) small-block V8 engine. 1968 was the first year for this powerplant in the Shelby GT350, which previously featured a 289-cubic-inch (4.7-liter) V8 . But even though the unit was a tad bigger, power dropped compared to the previous years.\nWhile the 289 V8 came with 306 horsepower on tap, the 302 V8-powered Shelby was rated at only 250 horsepower. That's because the 1968 mill had fewer racing parts than the more iconic K-code 289. The Cobra intake was pretty much the only feature that set it apart from the regular 302 Mustang mill. Shelby offered a Paxton supercharger upgrade, but those cars are very rare.\nOur host doesn't say much about the history of the car and whether this GT350 is up for grabs. American Mustangs is a classic car dealer specialising in high-performance Mustangs, so we may see it on their website soon (but it's not listed now). One of the two GT500KRs finished in this color was auctioned off in 2024 for $291,500 . Similarly, this GT350 is likely worth over a quarter-million bucks. Check it out in the video below.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nshelby gt350 1968 Shelby GT350 Ford Mustang Shelby Mustang classic car muscle car rare Grabber Orange\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Lamborghini Teases New Supercar Ahead of Next Week’s Debut, It’s Likely the Temerario Spyder",
